'''bench''' (bench), ''n.'' [E. dial. and Sc. also ''benk'', ''bink'', < ME. ''bench'', ''benk'', ''bynk'', < AS. ''benc'' (orig. *''banki'') = OS. ''bank'', ''benki'' = D. ''bank'' = OHG. ''banch'', MHG. G. ''bank'' = Icel. ''bekkr'' = Sw. ''bänk'' = Dan. ''bænk'', a bench: see ''bank''<sup>1</sup>, ''bank''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' A long seat, usually of board or plank, or of stone, differing from a stool in its greater length.
{{smaller block|He took his place once more on the ''bench'' at the inn door.
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 64.}}}}
'''2.''' The seat where judges sit in court; the seat of justice.
{{smaller block|To pluck down justice from your awful ''bench.''
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., v. 2.}}}}
Hence—'''3.''' The body of persons who sit as judges; the court: as, the case is to go before the full ''bench.''—'''4.''' A strong table on which carpenters or other mechanics do their work; a work-bench. {{smaller|In this sense ''bench'' forms an element in a number of compound words denoting tools used on a bench, such as ''bench-drill'', ''bench-hammer'', ''bench-plane.''}} '''5.''' The floor or ledge which supports muffles and retorts.—'''6.''' A platform or a series of elevated stalls or boxes on which animals are placed for exhibition, as at a dog-show.
{{smaller block|Excellence on the ''bench'' and excellence in the field may be two utterly diverse things.
{{right|''Forest and Stream'', XXII. 361.}}}}
'''7.''' In ''engin.'', a ledge left on the edge of a cutting in earthwork to strengthen it.—'''8.''' In ''geol.'' and

